n2.d2).\n- **Orders:** H1 2026 orders rose 11% year on year, led by utility and data-centre customers.\n- **Cancellations:** They were below 1%, so the backlog looks firm.\n- **Halbrook plant:** The fire in the coil-winding hall took the plant offline for nine weeks in Q2 2026. It restarted in August at full capacity (n2.d4).\n\n**What limits it**\n- **Revenue run-rate:** Recent quarterly revenue was C$905M, C$960M, C$980M and C$890M (2025Q3 to 2026Q2). That sums to about C$3.7B over the last four quarters. Reaching C$4.2B needs about C$1.05B per quarter, roughly 13% above that, or about 10% above the Q1 2026 level.\n- **Backlog conversion:** Covering guidance on paper is not the same as converting it to revenue. The extra revenue depends on output and delivery timing.\n- **Halbrook catch-up:** Management expects to recover the lost shipments only by mid-2027. That would add revenue in 2027, but it requires running above normal capacity. Any slip puts part of the guidance at risk.\n\n**Conclusion**\nThe disclosures imply 2027 revenue of roughly C$4.0–4.3B, with C$4.2B achievable but not comfortable. The main swing factor is how fast Halbrook catches up and how fast backlog converts into shipments. The industry figures that n3 is examining may cut against this: industry shipments fell 4% in Q2 2026, and Voltaris's market share dropped from 31% to 27%. That is a sign the order strength may not carry through to revenue.\n\nThe C$4.0–4.3B range is my own judgment from these few data points, not a modelled forecast.","ttft_ms":2388,"type":"result","duration_ms":26764,"uuid":"5d8760b8-e9f3-4b3b-88f6-5af31627422d","ttft_stream_ms":1456,"time_to_request_ms":104,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":19,"system_prompt":4,"resume_other":1,"process_user_input":2,"input_attachments":13,"input_hooks":2,"conversation_append":0,"transcript_persist":3,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":6,"init_frame_send":7,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":3,"message_prep":5,"model_config_wait":0,"au